         <div type="bio">
            <p>Portrait painter and illustrator. Born in Wales, he studied art in London, which continued as his principal home. In the 1920s and 1930s he made a name as a portraitist of the famous, including <name type="person" key="shaw56">Shaw</name>, <name type="person" key="ID_MISSING">Hardy</name>, and <name type="person" key="yeat65">Yeats</name>. In late October 1930 JJ sat for John in Paris and the artist produced a number of sanguine and pencil drawings. Some of these were used as frontispieces for JJ’s books, including <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">The Joyce Book</name> (1933), <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">Collected Poems</name> (1936), and <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">Stephen Hero</name> (1944). Though renowned for his exuberant and bohemian nature, in recent years John’s reputation as a painter has been overshadowed by that of his sister, <name type="person" key="ID_MISSING">Gwen John</name>. <hi rend="bold">William Brockman</hi>
                </p>
         </div>
